# Line endings, stated in the repository instead of in each developer's config.
#
# Every gate in this project asserts CRLF, and none of them could rely on the
# clone's settings:
#
#   qa_check.py             CRLF everywhere except FPC_DIRS, which it skips
#   lcl_svg_struct_check.py "the repo is pure CRLF" over LclSvgImageList.pas
#                           and SvgData.pas
#   f3_lfm_check.py         CRLF over all 55 generated .lfm files
#   f3_image_extract.py     byte identity between DataFrm.dfm and the generated
#                           ImageCollectionData.pas
#
# What actually satisfied them was each Windows developer's LOCAL
# core.autocrlf=true, which is recorded nowhere. The blobs are bare LF, so a CI
# checkout on Linux -- where autocrlf does nothing -- handed five gates bare LF
# and CI was red on every run since at least 2026-10-07, on files nobody had
# touched. Reproduced locally in a worktree with core.autocrlf=false before
# changing anything.
#
# So the conversion moves into the repo, where a clone can see it.
#
# The rule is deliberately one line and blanket. A narrower first attempt --
# scoped to qa_check.py's own ACTIVE_DIRS + TOUCHED_FILES + MANIFEST -- turned
# that one gate green and left four others red, because two of them require CRLF
# INSIDE the FPC subtree qa_check deliberately skips. Scoping to one gate's view
# is how three quarters of the problem gets missed.
#
# CRLF rather than "whatever autocrlf says" on purpose: it makes a Linux
# checkout byte-identical to a Windows one, and two checkouts of one commit must
# not differ in a way a byte-identity gate can see.
#
# text=auto leaves the 640 binary files alone, and there are no shell scripts in
# the tree that CRLF would break.
#
# This rewrites no blob. Files stay LF in the object store and convert on
# checkout; renormalising would have been a repo-wide rewrite of 2119 files,
# none of which is what this commit is about.
* text=auto eol=crlf